Q: Remove last text character for all elements with specific class A: <p>The <code>getElementsByClassName()</code> function returns a collection of HTML elements, not a single string.  Assuming that there is only one HTML element whose id is <code>gutentor-post-desc</code>, you may try something like the following:</p>
<pre><code>var elements = document.getElementsByClassName('gutentor-post-desc');

if (elements.length &gt; 0) {
    var text = elements[0].textContent;
    elements[0].textContent = text.substring(0, text.length - 1);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Objects.equals(null, null); should return false A: <p>To be fair, when I have</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String string = null;
Integer integer = null;
System.out.println(string == integer);
</code></pre>
<p>Java will produce a compiler error, saying this is an invalid comparison of incompatible types. So the idea of the compile-time type being relevant is not that odd. But as soon as we are widening the type to <code>Object</code>, as when passing it to <code>Objects.equals(Object,Object)</code>, the references still can be the same when being <code>null</code> and sometimes even when non-<code>null</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List&lt;String&gt;  list1 = Collections.emptyList();
List&lt;Integer&gt; list2 = Collections.emptyList();
System.out.println(list1 == list2)); // &lt;-- rejected by the compiler
System.out.println(list1 == (Object)list2); // &lt;-- true
</code></pre>
